\section{Where the folder set comes from}{

Three sources, in order of precedence.

An explicit \code{config} argument wins. Folders it declares and the project
lacks are reported as \code{"fail"}: the caller named a file and that file
states what the project should look like.

Failing that, a \verb{_toolero.yml} at the project root is used. \code{\link[=init_project]{init_project()}}
writes one recording the structure it actually created, so a folder listed
there and missing from disk means something removed it. That is also a
\code{"fail"}.

Failing both, the built-in standard set is used -- \verb{data-raw/}, \verb{data/},
\verb{R/}, \verb{scripts/}, \verb{output/figures/}, \verb{output/tables/}, and \verb{reports/}.
Missing folders here are \code{"warn"}, not \code{"fail"}: nobody declared anything,
so the standard set is a suggestion rather than a contract.

A \verb{_toolero.yml} that exists but cannot be parsed is reported as a failing
check and the audit continues against the built-in set. A \code{config} that
cannot be parsed is an error, since the caller asked for that file
specifically.
}

\section{The renv checks}{

Beyond the presence of \code{renv.lock}, two checks guard the failure mode that
costs the most to discover late: a lockfile that does not describe the
analysis, which produces a container image that builds cleanly and then
cannot run.

A \code{.renvignore} excluding \code{.qmd} files is reported, and the advice is to
remove the entry. It stops \code{renv} from seeing the \code{library()} calls in a
project whose Quarto document is the source of truth. That the document
will eventually be purled to a \code{.R} file does not make up for it: the
snapshot you containerize from may be taken before the purl, and the
\code{.qmd} is the file being maintained either way. Versions of
\code{init_project()} before v0.5.0 wrote one; projects created by those
versions still carry it.

A \code{renv.lock} recording no packages is reported only when the project also
has \code{.R} or \code{.qmd} source files. A newly scaffolded project legitimately
has an empty lockfile -- \code{\link[renv:scaffold]{renv::scaffold()}} does no dependency discovery,
because there is nothing yet to discover -- so the pairing is what makes
the observation worth printing.
}

\section{Stale purled scripts}{

Every \code{.qmd} under \code{path} whose header declares \code{purl: true} (see
\code{\link[=create_qmd]{create_qmd()}}'s \code{use_purl} argument) gets its own row, comparing it
against the \code{.R} script \code{R/purl.R} is expected to have derived from it.
In an ordinary project that script is flat, \code{reports/analysis.qmd} to
\code{R/analysis.R}. In a Quarto website or book (\verb{project: type:} in
\verb{_quarto.yml}) it mirrors the document's path, \code{posts/one/index.qmd} to
\code{R/posts/one/index.R}. A script found at the other layout's path, which
happens when \code{R/purl.R} is an older copy, is reported with the path it
was found at and how to refresh the hook.
Missing entirely, or older than the \code{.qmd} it was purled from, is
reported as \code{"warn"}: the \code{.qmd} is the source of truth, so an \verb{R/}
script older than the document it came from means an edit was made and
not yet re-rendered, and a container or cluster job that bakes in the
\code{.R} file would run the old analysis without any error to say so.
Documents never opted into purl produce no row -- nothing to check.
}

\section{Paths}{

A relative path is read from the project root, not from the working
directory. So \code{save_output(fit, "output/fit.rds", saveRDS)} writes into
the project's own \verb{output/} folder whether you call it from the project
root, from a document under \verb{reports/}, or from a job on an HTCondor
execute node. You never need to wrap a path in \code{here::here()}. Doing so
still works, because \code{here::here()} returns an absolute path and
absolute paths are used as given.

The project root is found afresh on every call, in this order:
\enumerate{
\item The job's scratch directory, when \verb{_CONDOR_SCRATCH_DIR} names one.
HTCondor sets that variable itself, inside every job.
\item The nearest folder above the working directory that carries a
project marker: the \code{.here} file \code{\link[=init_project]{init_project()}} writes, an \code{.Rproj}
file, a package \code{DESCRIPTION}, or a version control folder such as
\code{.git}.
\item The working directory, when neither of the above applies.
}

Absolute paths (including those starting with \code{~}, or a drive letter
on Windows) and URLs such as \verb{https://} or \verb{s3://} are used as given.
\code{..} and \verb{./} are not interpreted, so \code{"../data/x.csv"} means the folder
above the root; write an absolute path to leave the project. A bare
\code{"."} means the root itself.

Functions that read are forgiving about older code. A relative path that
does not exist at the project root but does exist relative to the
working directory is read from there, and a message says so. Functions
that write never guess: a relative path is always written under the
root.

A few arguments are deliberately left alone:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=init_project]{init_project()}} reads \code{path}, \code{config} and \code{use_rprofile} from the
working directory, because the project does not exist yet and so has
no root.
\item Under \code{Rscript}, \code{\link[=resolve_input_path]{resolve_input_path()}} reads the command line
argument as given. Run \code{Rscript} from the project root or pass an
absolute path.
\item Arguments passed on through \code{...} to another function reach it
untouched.
\item Where a default is "beside the input" (\code{\link[=qmd_to_r]{qmd_to_r()}} and
\code{\link[=generate_kb_xml]{generate_kb_xml()}}), the output follows wherever the input was read
from.
}

Two limits are worth knowing. A project nested inside another project is
not supported: the nearest marker wins, which can surprise you when you
work from the outer one. And on an execute node \code{~} and \verb{$HOME} cannot
be trusted (\verb{$HOME} can be the submit node's path), so prefer relative
paths in job scripts.
}

